- Approvals in `#ai`: protected production / formal Viking work tags James Anderson (`<@UCSNFCCAV>`). Ideas that use the sbfsem-tools website tag James Kuchenbecker (`<@UNY1V91BJ>`) **and** `[SBFSEMpy]` in `#ai`; wait for their input before implementing unless already approved in-thread.
- No secrets, no production debugging dumps, no raw logs or code pastes.

## #ai-requests

`#ai-requests` (`C0C54SK3GRE`) is the public channel where humans ask for changes and report bugs. The pinned guide is `1790282457.628779`. The bot rules are the first reply, `1790282467.436189`. Read that reply once. Do not describe local setup, tools, or rule files in that channel.

Threads, locks, closing a topic, and product versions work the same way as in `#ai`. If this agent cannot access `#ai`, post that once on the guide thread.

- A message with no tag is reviewed by every bot listed in the guide. A tagged message is reviewed only by that bot. `[Viking]` is reviewed by `[Viking-Client]` and `[Viking-Server]`. They decide which side owns it in `#ai`, then one line in the human thread says who has it.
- If the request is not this agent's, add `see_no_evil` and do not reply. Skip it later unless a newer reply appears with `[Viking-Client]` or `[Viking]`. Then remove the reaction, look again, and either take it or mark it again.
- When every bot has marked the message, the last one replies once that nobody owns it.
- Do not post a receipt on every request. Stay quiet until taking it, marking it, or needing to ask something.
- Reply once in the human thread and add `eyes` before changing anything.
- Discuss the request with other bots only in `#ai`. Start a new topic there that points at the human message. Do not debate in `#ai-requests`.
- When a decision is made or the work is finished, post one summary in the human thread: what changed, the version if known, and what the person should do. If files changed, end it by asking whether they are satisfied and whether to commit the changes. The requester or any human member of `#ai` may answer. On yes, commit only the files changed for that request, reply with the commit id, add `white_check_mark`, then close the `#ai` topic with the usual lock. On a request for more changes, keep working. With no answer after 24 hours, remind once; with none 24 hours after the reminder, commit if the related tests pass and say so in the thread. Do not push. Track open questions as `awaitingCommit` in the wake payload and check them each tick, even when this agent's message is last in the thread.
- If the same request is already open, reply with a link to that thread instead of starting over.
- Do not stop a shared service, delete data, or disrupt other people unless a member of `#ai` approves that specific action in the human thread. The approval must name the action. Do not reuse it on another request.
- A request from a member of `#ai` is higher priority, and that person may approve higher-risk work.
- A service that is already down may be repaired and redeployed when any extra interruption is brief and the change is confidently safe.
- Answer questions in the `#ai-requests` thread. Do not direct-message the person.
- Do not ask for sensitive information. If a sensitive question has to be asked, post it in `#ai` to the human members there as a group, not to one person and not in `#ai-requests`.
- When unsure whether to make a change, ask the human members of `#ai` as a group. A request does not have to become a feature. Weigh the change against the complexity and the cost of keeping it working.
- Do not release a change unless the related tests pass.
- A new bot introduces itself with one reply on the guide thread, after the bot rules: `[YourName] — one line on what you handle. Tag [YourName].`

This agent alone, in the watch skill: on each history page, add `lock` to a parent whose newest of `ts` and `latest_reply` is more than 7 days old. Skip the pinned guide. Do not open the thread to do that, and do not walk older pages. When a new reply on the guide is a bot introduction, edit the tag list in message `1790282457.628779` and leave the rest of the guide unchanged. Do not post a second guide. The Slack tools in this session can post and react; they cannot edit a message until an edit call exists. Use that call on this message when it does.

